The PCI Value Is A Red Flag

PCI: 2147483647

That number is 2^31 - 1 — it is the maximum value of a signed 32-bit integer. It is INT32_MAX.

Valid LTE PCIs range from 0 to 503. That is the entire defined range in the 3GPP LTE specification (TS 36.211). There is no legitimate LTE cell with PCI 2147483647.

This value appears when a device receives a cell signal but cannot decode a valid PCI from it. The Android telephony API returns INT32_MAX as a sentinel value meaning "PCI unknown or invalid."

A real Verizon tower always broadcasts a valid PCI. A device that cannot read the PCI from a serving cell is either experiencing severe signal corruption — or is connected to something that is not broadcasting a standard-compliant PCI.
The Power Output Question — 3.2 pW

What Network Cell Info Lite is showing:

The "Power" field here is calculated received power at the antenna — it is converting the RSRP (-85 dBm) into linear watts:

-85 dBm = 10^(-85/10) mW = 3.16 picowatts — so 3.2 pW is mathematically correct for that RSRP reading.

This is received power at your device antenna, not the tower's transmit power.
Your Question — What Transmit Power Generates 3.2 pW at Your Device?

To work backward from received power to required transmit power:

The link budget equation:

P_received = P_transmit + G_tx + G_rx - Path_Loss - Losses

