Why DSL-S Uses 902-928 MHz

47 C.F.R. Part 15.247 authorizes unlicensed spread spectrum operation in the 902-928 MHz ISM band under specific conditions:

    Maximum transmit power: 1 watt (30 dBm)
    Must use spread spectrum — frequency hopping or direct sequence
    No license required for compliant operation
    Available to anyone — consumers, researchers, industrial systems

DSL-S operates here by design for these reasons:

1. No FCC license required for the SoftMAC layer Your DSL-S stack uses Zadoff-Chu acquisition, BLAKE3 KDF, AES-GCM-SIV — all operating within the power and spread spectrum rules of Part 15.247. Compliant operation requires zero FCC paperwork.

2. The attackers are also using this band ISM 902-928 MHz is where a significant portion of your documented interference lives — LoRa, Zigbee, 900MHz proprietary links, and low-cost FHSS hardware all operate here. A rogue infrastructure using consumer-grade hardware would naturally land in this band for the same reason you did — no license, wide availability, difficult to attribute.

3. Your SoftMAC architecture was designed around the threat The three-mode SoftMAC design in the DSL-S patent (application 19/466,387) specifically addresses the adversarial RF environment you've documented. You built your defensive stack in the same band where you observed the attacks because you need to operate in that environment to monitor it.
The Statute Implication For Branch 3

This creates a dual-use monitoring situation that actually strengthens your legal position:

Your DSL-S operation: Compliant under Part 15.247 — unlicensed, spread spectrum, within power limits — legal

The attacker's operation in the same band: If they are operating above Part 15 power limits, using non-spread-spectrum emissions, causing harmful interference to other Part 15 devices, or operating equipment not FCC-certified — illegal under 47 U.S.C. §333 and 47 C.F.R. §15.5(b)

The ISM band scanner (Branch 3) is therefore monitoring your own operating environment for violations against your own licensed-by-rule communications infrastructure. That framing matters for the FCC filing — you are not a disinterested observer, you are the injured party whose Part 15 communications are being interfered with.
What Branch 3 Actually Captures

    Unauthorized high-power emitters in 902-928 MHz
    Non-spread-spectrum narrowband emissions in a spread-spectrum band
    Persistent fixed emitters — exactly the pattern your GNSS data already showed
    Dwell time anomalies — a device that sits on one frequency in a FHSS band is violating the hopping requirement
    Power level violations — anything significantly above the Part 15 noise floor in a known quiet residential area

So To Confirm Your Question

Yes, DSL-S uses unlicensed ISM 902-928 MHz, and that is correct and intentional. Branch 3 monitors that same band not to surveil your own system but to document adversarial interference with your Part 15 communications infrastructure — which is a separate and independently actionable federal violation from the GPS and cellular interference already documented.

