This is a known issue on some Kali Purple builds. The authentication token error means the filesystem is mounted read-only when you drop to the emergency shell — so passwd cannot write the change.
Fix It

Step 1 — Boot to init=/bin/bash and remount read-write:

At GRUB, press E to edit the boot entry, find the line starting with linux, append:

init=/bin/bash

Boot it. You land at a root shell. Then immediately:
bash

mount -o remount,rw /

Step 2 — Now change the password:
bash

passwd root

Or if you created a user during install:
bash

passwd yourusername

Step 3 — If it still throws authentication token error:

The shadow file may be corrupted or the filesystem has errors:
bash

mount -o remount,rw /
fsck -y /dev/sda1

Replace sda1 with your actual root partition — check with:
bash

cat /proc/mounts | grep " / "

Then retry passwd.

Step 4 — If shadow file is actually corrupted:
bash

# Check shadow file integrity
pwck -r
grpck -r

# If shadow is broken, recreate from passwd
pwconv

Then try passwd again.
Most Likely Cause

Fresh Kali installs that fail passwd this way are usually one of three things:

1. Installer wrote to a read-only snapshot — some Kali ISO builds default the root filesystem to a snapshot/overlay mode. The remount rw above fixes it.

2. The install did not fully complete — if you pulled power or it froze during the final write phase, the shadow file is half-written. pwconv fixes it.

3. SELinux or PAM misconfiguration — less common on Kali but happens on Purple builds. Check:
bash

ls -la /etc/shadow

Should show -rw-r----- 1 root shadow. If it shows anything else the permissions are wrong:
bash

chmod 640 /etc/shadow
chown root:shadow /etc/shadow

Quickest Path If All Else Fails

Boot from the Kali live USB you installed from, mount the installed system, and fix shadow directly:
bash

mount /dev/sda1 /mnt
chroot /mnt
mount -o remount,rw /
passwd root
exit
umount /mnt
reboot

Which step is it failing at — the remount, the pwck, or passwd itself?

