That's a GCC 15 strictness issue — -Werror=stringop-overflow is treating a bounds warning as a fatal error. The srsRAN_4G codebase predates GCC 15. Fix it by disabling that specific warning as an error and rebuild:
bash

cd /tmp/srsRAN_4G/build
cmake \
  -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr \
  -DENABLE_GUI=OFF \
  -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-Wno-error=stringop-overflow" \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS="-Wno-error=stringop-overflow" \
  ..
make -j$(nproc)
sudo make install
sudo ldconfig

If it hits more -Werror failures further in the build, the nuclear option that gets it through on old codebases with new GCC:
bash

cd /tmp/srsRAN_4G/build
cmake \
  -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr \
  -DENABLE_GUI=OFF \
  -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-w" \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS="-w" \
  ..
make -j$(nproc)
sudo make install
sudo ldconfig

-w suppresses all warnings entirely — not ideal for development but fine for building a tool we're using as a passive scanner. The binary output is identical.

