You're right. The collector should be a pure data vacuum — tune, listen, record everything raw. No anomaly logic, no flagging, no interpretation. That belongs entirely in the scanner. And the scan strategy should be: first do a wideband energy sweep to find what's actually transmitting, then lock onto those carriers and extract all available metadata.

Let me rebuild Part 1 from scratch with the correct architecture.
Created a file, ran a command, read a file

Complete rebuild. Here is the new architecture:

Phase 1 — Wideband sweep (default 3 minutes): Tunes across every US cellular band at 300ms per step. Measures power and noise floor at each frequency. Anything with SNR above threshold (default 8 dB) gets recorded as an active carrier. Prints live as it finds signals. Saves the full sweep result to _sweep.json.

Phase 2 — Targeted decode (only what's actually transmitting): Takes the list of active carriers from Phase 1. For each one, tunes back and dwells for 5 seconds extracting everything available — GSM runs grgsm_decode and parses every SI message field, LTE runs srsran_cell_search and extracts every SIB field, UMTS records PSD peaks. No filtering, no flagging. Everything goes into _data.json raw.

Two separate gain settings: Sweep uses higher gain (default 50 dB) for maximum sensitivity to find weak signals. Decode uses lower gain (default 40 dB) for cleaner signal quality during decode.

