ARFCN 1538 does not map to a standard GSM frequency — which is actually useful because it tells you what the device is doing internally to generate that ARFCN number.

CellMapper reads ARFCN directly from what the baseband chipset reports. When the baseband sees a GSM burst it cannot place in a standard band, it still reports the ARFCN it decoded from the broadcast. So ARFCN 1538 is real — the question is what actual RF frequency it corresponds to.

