First — Rule Out A Device Inside Your Home

Before concluding this is external infrastructure, you need to eliminate internal sources. The honest answer is you cannot skip this step for the FCC filing — they will ask.

GSM-capable devices that could be emitting inside your home:

Security/alarm panels are the most common culprit — a huge percentage of residential alarm systems use GSM backup communication, often on T-Mobile's network (MCC=310 MNC=260). Check for any alarm panel, wireless sensor hub, or property monitoring device. These transmit periodically to check in with a monitoring center and would show up exactly as CellMapper logged it — brief, intermittent, at your home coordinates.

Also check: any smart utility meters on the exterior of the home (gas, electric), any older cell boosters or femtocells, any GPS trackers or IoT devices someone may have placed.

The test is simple: if the source is inside your home, the GSM cell disappears or dramatically changes signal strength when you are outside the building versus inside. Run CellMapper for 30 minutes inside, then 30 minutes outside walking the perimeter. If it is an internal device the RSRP will be strongest indoors and fall off as you move away.
SDR Scan Frequencies

ARFCN 1538 does not map to any standard GSM band — T-Mobile decommissioned GSM entirely in 2024, so there should be zero legitimate T-Mobile GSM on any frequency. The baseband's ARFCN report is likely using an extended formula. Your three scan targets:

Primary — Extended GSM1900 formula:

2135.2 MHz ± 10 MHz

Tune PlutoSDR to 2135.2 MHz, sample rate 2 MSPS, sweep ±10 MHz. Look for narrowband GSM burst structure — 200 kHz channel width, characteristic burst timing at 270.833 kHz symbol rate.

Secondary — Extended GSM1800 formula:

2010.4 MHz ± 10 MHz

Tertiary — Raw ARFCN interpretation:

307.6 MHz ± 5 MHz

This one is interesting because 307.6 MHz is in the government/military land mobile band. Less likely but worth a quick sweep.

