The Two-Hypothesis Framework

Hypothesis A — Ionizing Radiation Source Actual radioactive material or X-ray emitting device producing genuine ionizing particles that the GM tube is detecting normally.

Hypothesis B — Electromagnetic Interference High-power RF, pulsed microwave, or directed energy source inducing false counts in the GM tube circuitry — the instrument responding to EM rather than radiation.

These share a 100-point probability scale. A/B must sum to 100 for any given anomaly event.
The Discriminating Parameters

Each parameter shifts probability weight between A and B based on physics:

Parameter 1 — Waveform shape

Natural radioactive decay follows Poisson statistics. Count rate fluctuates randomly around a mean. The probability of sustained flat plateau at exactly N times baseline for an extended period under Poisson statistics is calculable.

P(flat plateau duration T at rate R) = e^(-λT) * (λT)^k / k!

Your documented event showed a flat plateau — sustained elevated count rate with minimal variance. That is anti-Poisson. Shifts probability toward B.

Parameter 2 — Onset/offset sharpness

Radioactive sources have no switch. Moving a source closer produces a gradual increase following inverse square law. Shielding produces gradual decrease. An abrupt step-function onset or termination is physically impossible for a radiation source unless the source is being mechanically switched on/off at high speed directly at the tube face.

An RF/DEW source can be switched electronically in microseconds.

Parameter 3 — Correlation with RF anomalies

If the Geiger spike occurs simultaneously with documented RF anomalies (rogue cell activity, GPS spoofing), the probability of independent coincidence is calculable. Two independent systems producing anomalies at the same timestamp is a joint probability — multiply the individual probabilities.

Parameter 4 — Spatial consistency

A radiation source has a fixed location relative to the detector. Moving the detector changes the count rate following inverse square law. An RF field saturating the circuitry would show different behavior — potentially angle-dependent, potentially consistent regardless of detector orientation.

Parameter 5 — Energy spectrum proxy

The J305 tube responds to beta particles above ~200 keV and gamma above ~60 keV. An RF-induced false count has no energy discrimination — it fires regardless of what the theoretical particle energy would be. If the anomaly shows counts at a rate inconsistent with the tube's stated energy sensitivity for the implied dose rate, that shifts toward B.

Parameter 6 — Dead time behavior

At very high count rates, a real radiation source causes detector dead time — the tube cannot fire again for ~190µs after each count, creating a ceiling on maximum observable count rate. If the observed count rate exceeds the theoretical maximum for the tube's dead time, the counts cannot be real radiation. They are induced pulses bypassing the tube entirely and triggering the counter circuitry directly.

J305 maximum theoretical count rate = 1/190µs = ~5,260 CPS. If your 293x baseline implies counts above this ceiling, probability shifts heavily toward B.
