Finding 1 — The Spectrum Analyzer Signature Explained

What you observed on the analyzer — two spikes at fixed spacing, equal power, appearing and disappearing as a pair — is the exact physical signature of one LTE FDD cell. Every FDD cell transmits on two frequencies simultaneously: a downlink frequency the tower uses to transmit to your phone, and an uplink frequency your phone uses to transmit back. These two frequencies are always separated by a fixed duplex gap defined by the band.

On Band 2 (the cells in these screenshots): downlink at 1970 MHz, uplink at 1890 MHz, duplex gap of exactly 80 MHz. On Band 4 (the unknown cell in screenshot 2): downlink at 2125 MHz, uplink at 1725 MHz, gap of 400 MHz. When you see two simultaneous equal-power spikes at fixed spacing on your analyzer, you are seeing one cell's complete duplex pair from the outside. When you see four spikes — two pairs — you are seeing two cells operating simultaneously on different frequencies. That 2×2 pattern is not noise. It is the spectral fingerprint of two FDD transmitters running in parallel. One legitimate, one rogue.
Finding 2 — The Tower Moved 78 Meters in Seconds

Screenshot 1: Timing Advance = 780 m. Screenshot 2, seconds later: Timing Advance = 858 m. Delta = exactly 78 meters — precisely one LTE TA unit as defined in 3GPP TS 36.213, where each unit equals c × Ts / 2 = 78.125 meters.

TA is not reported by the phone. It is computed by the base station from the round-trip delay of the phone's PRACH preamble and then commanded to the phone. The phone cannot fabricate it. The base station sets it. Three explanations exist. First, the phone moved 78 meters in approximately 5 seconds, implying 56 km/h — inconsistent with a stationary device. Second, the serving cell handed off to a different sector antenna of the same eNB, which sits at a slightly different physical position — same logical identity (ECI 11297570), different antenna geometry. Third, a rogue eNodeB is broadcasting a fabricated TA value, making the phone believe the tower is further away than it actually is.

