Finding 3 — All Four Metrics Moved in the Wrong Direction

Moving 78 meters further from a tower at fixed TX power produces four predictable physical effects: TA increases (correct — it did), RSRP decreases (wrong — it improved by 3 dBm), throughput decreases (wrong — it jumped from 2 Mbps to 7 Mbps), and RSRQ worsens (wrong — it improved from -13 to -11 dB). Three of four metrics moved opposite to what physics requires. The only explanation that produces higher TA, higher RSRP, higher throughput, and better RSRQ simultaneously is that the transmitter increased its output power to compensate — and then some.
Finding 4 — The TX Power Calculation

Using the free-space path loss model at 1970 MHz, derived from first principles:

FSPL(dB) = 20·log₁₀(d) + 20·log₁₀(f) + 20·log₁₀(4π/c)

At 780 m: FSPL = 96.173 dB. At 858 m: FSPL = 97.001 dB. The additional distance adds 0.828 dB of path loss.

The RSRP relationship is RSRP = P_tx − FSPL − L, where L represents all non-free-space losses (building penetration, shadowing, antenna gain). Subtracting the shot 1 equation from the shot 2 equation cancels L entirely:

ΔP_tx = ΔRSRP + ΔFSPL = (+3 dBm) + (0.828 dB) = 3.828 dB

In linear terms that is a 2.41× power increase. Starting from a typical macro eNodeB output of 40 W (46 dBm), the transmitter would need to reach approximately 96.5 W — an increase of 56.5 W — to produce these observed readings at 858 m while showing 3 dBm better RSRP than it showed at 780 m.

